.nh
.TH "PVEFORGE" "1" "Sep 2026" "pveforge" "pveforge manual"

.SH NAME
pveforge-network-bridge-destroy - Stage and commit the removal of a node-level network bridge


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpveforge network bridge destroy <target-id> <iface> [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Destroy a node-level network interface (typically a bridge) via PVE's own
two-phase stage/commit model: the removal is staged (DELETE
/nodes/{node}/network/{iface}), independently guard-checked, and only then
committed (PUT /nodes/{node}/network), which is the single call that
actually triggers ifupdown2's live reload.

.PP
--management-bridge is REQUIRED, with no default, and this command has NO
--force override for the guard check — see "network bridge create --help"
for the full rationale, which applies identically here.

.PP
Removing a live bridge can disconnect any VM currently attached to it —
there is no compiler- or PVE-side check for that here, so confirm nothing
depends on iface before running this.

.PP
If the change is made but its result cannot be re-read afterwards, a
one-line warning is printed on stderr; stdout is unchanged and the exit
status is still 0.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for destroy

.PP
\fB--lock-wait\fP=0s
	how long to wait for another pveforge process's lock on this object before giving up (0 = the 15m0s default; at most 1h0m0s). The default exceeds the 10m0s ceiling on waiting for a PVE task because a legitimate lock holder may be in such a wait the whole time

.PP
\fB--management-bridge\fP=""
	the node's own management bridge (e.g. vmbr0) — required, no default; see this command's --help for why

.PP
\fB--roster\fP=""
	path to the roster file (overrides PVEFORGE_ROSTER and the default ./pveforge.toml)


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpveforge-network-bridge(1)\fP
